<Doc>
    <Code code={`bunx @abide/abide scaffold ledger
cd ledger
bun install
bun run dev`} />

    <Section title="What it writes">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/index.abide</code></td><td>the page at <code>/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/layout.abide</code></td><td>the root layout: the document its pages are served in, and the <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> holding the lifecycle hooks</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/getGreeting.ts</code></td><td>a handler at <code>/getGreeting</code>, which the page imports and calls</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/tests/getGreeting.test.ts</code></td><td>a test calling the same handler, run by <code>bun run test</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>package.json</code>, <code>tsconfig.json</code>, <code>biome.json</code>, <code>bunfig.toml</code></td><td>the toolchain, configured — tests included</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>#src/*</code> is the one import alias, mapping onto <code>src/</code>. Grow the app
        by subject — a <code>src/billing/</code> with its own pages and handlers, a <code>lib</code>
        for its components and a <code>server</code> — and its pages answer below
        <code>/billing</code>, with no file moved to make room.</p>

        <p>Nothing generated is a framework file you are asked not to edit. The layout is a real
        component writing a real HTML document, and the page is a component too.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/configuration')}>Configuration</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/give-pages-the-same-chrome')}>Layouts</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`abide --help` prints usage">
        <p><code>abide help</code>, <code>abide -h</code> and <code>abide --help</code> all print
        usage <strong>generated from the command list</strong>. It is generated rather than written,
        so a command that exists is a command the help mentions and there is no second place for one
        to go missing. A mistyped command names the one it was near, and <code>abide --version</code>
        prints the version installed.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/cli')}>CLI</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Where to go from the scaffold">
        <p>The scaffolded page holds a reactive value and shows it, and greets you through a handler
        it imports — the same function <code>bun run test</code> calls. That is the whole of the first
        thing to learn — a value, and markup that follows it — and everything else on this site is a
        name for something you will want once that is boring.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/start/your-first-page')}>First page</a> ·
        <a href={url('/start/what-abide-is-for')}>Why abide</a></p>
    </Section>

